WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:05.350
The quest to understand the laws of nature,
at the deepest possible level has been maybe

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:10.530
one of the noblest and most consequential
aspirations of human beings for centuries

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:17.060
now. And in fundamental physics we’ve understood
that to do this we have to go to the extremes,

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:21.949
so we study the laws of nature — what the
universe looks like on the longest possible

00:00:21.949 --> 00:00:27.730
distances, the largest possible distances
in cosmology. But also at the very very smallest

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:32.359
possible distances, where we discover that
matter is made up out of elementary particles

00:00:32.359 --> 00:00:39.719
that interact with each other governed by
fundamental forces. And rather surprisingly

00:00:39.719 --> 00:00:43.329
and very importantly, in the last hundred
years we’ve learned that what seems to be

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:49.350
a dizzying array of very different phenomenon
turn out to be much more closely related to

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:54.780
each other than would appear at first sight.
And that the essential simplicity and unity

00:00:54.780 --> 00:01:00.860
and really beauty of the laws of nature only
reveal themselves when you study nature under

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:06.880
a microscope and you look at what’s happening
at very short distances or at very high energies.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:13.340
So we’ve been going to this short distance
frontier, high energy frontier, for a century

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:19.959
now and there’s absolutely no reason to
stop. In fact, a machine very similar to the

00:01:19.959 --> 00:01:24.619
machine that’s being talked about now was
being entertained in the United States already

00:01:24.619 --> 00:01:30.499
in the early 1990s — a super conducting
super collider. The U.S., tragically, decided

00:01:30.499 --> 00:01:35.149
not to proceed with that project, but it’s
something we know how to do. The technology

00:01:35.149 --> 00:01:41.479
exists. The know-how exists. And the scale
of resources that we’re talking about is

00:01:41.479 --> 00:01:47.609
not beyond the pale of similar projects in
the past. So that’s the big picture answer

00:01:47.609 --> 00:01:52.789
to this question. We need to build this collider
because we can and because it represents the

00:01:52.789 --> 00:01:57.182
frontier of what we’ve been doing and studying
the fundamental laws of physics for centuries now.

